1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,642
Error messages look scary, but every
Python error follows the same pattern.

2
00:00:05,142 --> 00:00:09,245
Once you know where to look, you can fix
most of them in under a minute.

3
00:00:09,745 --> 00:00:17,620
In this video, we'll read three real
errors together: a name error, a type
error, and a longer traceback that runs
through two functions.

4
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,456
Here's a tiny program that averages three
test scores. Let's run it.

5
00:00:22,956 --> 00:00:29,123
That block of red and white text is called
a traceback. The trick is to read it from
the bottom up.

6
00:00:29,623 --> 00:00:36,727
Python prints the oldest step at the top,
and the most recent one at the bottom, so
the bottom is where things broke.

7
00:00:37,227 --> 00:00:42,818
The last line tells you what went wrong.
Name error. The name t o t l is not
defined.

8
00:00:43,318 --> 00:00:46,984
Python even suggests a fix. Did you mean,
total?

9
00:00:47,484 --> 00:00:53,368
Now go up one line. It shows the exact
code, and the little arrows point right at
the problem.

10
00:00:53,868 --> 00:00:58,144
Above that, you get the file name and the
line number. Line three.

11
00:00:58,644 --> 00:01:02,519
So it's just a typo. Let's fix it, and run
again.

12
00:01:03,019 --> 00:01:05,980
A new error. That's progress, not failure.

13
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:11,026
Fixing one problem lets Python run
further, and find the next one.

14
00:01:11,526 --> 00:01:18,402
Bottom line first, again. Type error. Can
only concatenate str, not float, to str.

15
00:01:18,902 --> 00:01:23,491
In plain words, you can't glue text and a
number together with a plus sign.

16
00:01:23,991 --> 00:01:26,556
The arrows point at the plus, on line
four.

17
00:01:27,056 --> 00:01:33,361
One fix: pass both values to print,
separated by a comma. Print adds the space
for you.

18
00:01:33,861 --> 00:01:35,332
Run it, and it works.

19
00:01:35,832 --> 00:01:41,027
You could also convert the number with s t
r, but the comma is simpler, and easier to
read.

20
00:01:41,527 --> 00:01:48,153
Longer programs give longer tracebacks.
Here, report calls average, and we call
report twice.

21
00:01:48,653 --> 00:01:49,465
Let's run it.

22
00:01:49,965 --> 00:01:55,270
First, the good news. The first line
printed fine, so the call for Aarav
worked.

23
00:01:55,770 --> 00:02:02,324
Also notice that recent Python versions
underline the exact part of each line,
with tildes and carets.

24
00:02:02,824 --> 00:02:06,817
Now the bottom line. Zero division error.
Division by zero.

25
00:02:07,317 --> 00:02:15,112
Each pair of lines above it is one step in
the chain of calls. The lowest one is
where it crashed: inside average, on line
two.

26
00:02:15,612 --> 00:02:21,058
The top one is where it started. Line
eight, the call for Meera, with an empty
list.

27
00:02:21,558 --> 00:02:28,936
An empty list has a length of zero, so we
divided by zero. The real bug isn't in
average. It's the data we passed in.

28
00:02:29,436 --> 00:02:33,493
Let's guard against it. If the list is
empty, return zero.

29
00:02:33,993 --> 00:02:36,672
Run it again. Both reports work now.

30
00:02:37,172 --> 00:02:42,201
So, to read any Python error: start at the
last line for the type and the message.

31
00:02:42,701 --> 00:02:49,630
Then go up to find the file and the line.
And in a long traceback, follow the chain
until you reach your own code.

32
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:56,332
And if you're ever stuck, search for the
last line of the error. Someone has almost
certainly seen it before.

33
00:02:56,832 --> 00:03:03,471
Now pause the video, break the program on
purpose, and see if you can predict the
fix from the traceback.
